  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 344 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nConditions are looking unsettled for the coming weekend. Saturday, \nupper-level low pressure will slowly be tracking east-southeastward \nand be entering western Wyoming by late in the day. At the surface, \na rather moist easterly upslope flow will aid in the the development \nof late afternoon convection across the western High Plains. \nInitially, isolated severe storms will be possible, particularly in \nthe early stages of development across the Panhandle. During the \nevening and overnight, the upper-low will slowly wobble eastward \nacross Wyoming. Favorable downstream upper-jet dynamics will aid in \nascent increasing across Nebraska through the night. Convection \nshould develop/move eastward off the higher terrain to the west and \nmove eastward across the area overnight. The overall severe threat \neast of the Panhandle looks low, but locally heavy rainfall could \nmaterialize. PWATS greater than 1.3\" will be in place as mid-level \nsub-tropical moisture is pulled northeastward across the region \nahead of the low.  \n\nSunday, the center of the upper low will track slowly across eastern \nWyoming and into southwest South Dakota and northwest Nebraska by \nSunday evening. Continued chances for at least scattered showers and \na few thunderstorms will continue across the area in advance of the \nlow. The low slowly weakens and fills as it crosses southern South \nDakota and northern Nebraska Monday.  \n\nBy Tuesday into Wednesday, another upper trough moves onshore the \nwest coast. An area of sub-tropical moisture will be located across \nthe desert southwest into Mexico. This appears to be pulled \nnortheastward across the area as the southwest upper flow \nstrengthens ahead of the west coast trough. This could bring another \nchance for rainfall to the area later Tuesday into Wednesday.",
